{"module":"voting","voice":"male","duration":290.2,"segments":[{"el":"title","text":"The fight for the vote","start":0.0,"end":2.04,"words":[[0.05,0.31],[0.31,0.83],[0.83,1.09],[1.09,1.35],[1.35,1.88]]},{"el":"intro","text":"The people who decide whether discriminatory 911 calls stay legal are elected.","start":2.84,"end":8.86,"words":[[2.89,3.04],[3.04,3.4],[3.4,3.55],[3.55,3.99],[3.99,4.28],[4.28,5.3],[5.3,6.68],[6.68,6.97],[6.97,7.27],[7.27,7.63],[7.63,7.7],[7.7,8.29]]},{"el":"intro","text":"That is why the vote has been fought over so fiercely for 150 years, and why it is the most powerful tool for passing the CAREN Act.","start":9.21,"end":18.63,"words":[[9.25,9.49],[9.49,9.65],[9.65,9.89],[9.89,10.06],[10.06,10.38],[10.38,10.62],[10.62,10.86],[10.86,11.11],[11.11,11.43],[11.43,11.67],[11.67,12.16],[12.16,12.32],[12.32,13.69],[13.69,14.09],[14.38,14.62],[14.62,14.86],[14.86,15.02],[15.02,15.18],[15.18,15.35],[15.35,15.75],[15.75,16.32],[16.32,16.56],[16.56,16.72],[16.72,17.12],[17.12,17.29],[17.29,17.69],[17.69,18.01]]},{"el":"h0","text":"Won: Reconstruction","start":18.98,"end":20.56,"words":[[19.03,19.29],[19.52,20.35]]},{"el":"s0","text":"The 15th Amendment (1870) barred denying the vote because of race.","start":21.26,"end":26.21,"words":[[21.31,21.45],[21.45,21.94],[21.94,22.57],[22.57,23.42],[23.66,23.87],[23.87,24.36],[24.36,24.5],[24.5,24.78],[24.78,25.13],[25.13,25.27],[25.27,25.62]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Within a few years, Black voters elected about 2,000 Black officeholders across the South, from sheriffs to state legislators.","start":26.56,"end":35.23,"words":[[26.61,26.98],[26.98,27.12],[27.12,27.34],[27.34,27.71],[27.97,28.26],[28.26,28.7],[28.7,29.21],[29.21,29.58],[29.58,30.24],[30.24,30.53],[30.53,31.34],[31.34,31.7],[31.7,31.85],[31.85,32.22],[32.47,32.77],[32.77,33.21],[33.21,33.35],[33.35,33.72],[33.72,34.67]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Hiram Revels of Mississippi became the first Black U.S. Senator in 1870.","start":35.58,"end":41.67,"words":[[35.62,36.1],[36.1,36.57],[36.57,36.73],[36.73,37.37],[37.37,37.84],[37.84,38.0],[38.0,38.32],[38.32,38.63],[38.63,39.03],[39.43,39.9],[39.9,40.06],[40.06,41.09]]},{"el":"s0","text":"(See the module on Reconstruction.)","start":42.02,"end":44.23,"words":[[42.06,42.19],[42.19,42.32],[42.32,42.72],[42.72,42.85],[42.85,43.77]]},{"el":"h1","text":"Taken: poll taxes, tests and terror","start":45.13,"end":48.43,"words":[[45.17,45.75],[46.04,46.37],[46.37,46.94],[47.23,47.64],[47.64,47.89],[47.89,48.22]]},{"el":"s1","text":"When Reconstruction ended, Southern states rewrote their constitutions to get around the 15th Amendment without naming race.","start":49.13,"end":56.83,"words":[[49.18,49.38],[49.38,50.27],[50.27,50.67],[50.91,51.25],[51.25,51.66],[51.66,52.07],[52.07,52.27],[52.27,53.15],[53.15,53.29],[53.29,53.49],[53.49,53.9],[53.9,54.03],[54.03,54.51],[54.51,55.12],[55.12,55.53],[55.53,55.93],[55.93,56.27]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Mississippi led in 1890 with poll taxes, literacy tests and an “understanding clause” that let white registrars pass whom they chose.","start":57.18,"end":66.74,"words":[[57.23,57.88],[57.88,58.12],[58.12,58.28],[58.28,59.18],[59.18,59.42],[59.42,59.75],[59.75,60.31],[60.6,61.25],[61.25,61.65],[61.65,61.9],[61.9,62.06],[62.06,62.95],[62.95,63.28],[63.28,63.52],[63.52,63.77],[63.77,64.09],[64.09,64.9],[64.9,65.15],[65.15,65.39],[65.39,65.63],[65.63,66.12]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Other tools were “grandfather clauses” (struck down in 1915) and all-white primaries (struck down in 1944).","start":67.09,"end":75.01,"words":[[67.13,67.37],[67.37,67.68],[67.68,67.83],[67.83,68.53],[68.53,68.99],[68.99,69.38],[69.38,69.69],[69.69,69.84],[69.84,70.85],[71.12,71.35],[71.35,71.82],[71.82,72.51],[72.51,72.9],[72.9,73.21],[73.21,73.37],[73.37,74.45]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Violence enforced the rest: in Ocoee, Florida, in 1920, Black residents who tried to vote were massacred.","start":75.36,"end":84.37,"words":[[75.4,76.06],[76.06,76.55],[76.55,76.71],[76.71,77.12],[77.41,77.57],[77.57,77.98],[78.27,78.93],[79.21,79.38],[79.38,80.53],[80.81,81.14],[81.14,81.88],[81.88,82.04],[82.04,82.45],[82.45,82.62],[82.62,82.94],[82.94,83.11],[83.11,83.76]]},{"el":"s1","text":"The results were stark.","start":84.72,"end":86.24,"words":[[84.84,84.97],[84.97,85.4],[85.4,85.52],[85.52,85.83]]},{"el":"s1","text":"In Louisiana, registered Black voters fell from about 130,000 in 1896 to about 1,300 by 1904.","start":86.59,"end":94.02,"words":[[86.64,86.75],[86.75,87.29],[87.48,87.96],[87.96,88.18],[88.18,88.5],[88.5,88.66],[88.66,88.88],[88.88,89.14],[89.14,90.38],[90.38,90.49],[90.49,91.3],[91.3,91.4],[91.4,91.67],[91.67,92.75],[92.75,92.91],[92.91,93.56]]},{"el":"s1","text":"At Alabama's 1901 constitutional convention, the convention president said its purpose was to “establish white supremacy in this State.”","start":94.37,"end":103.71,"words":[[94.41,94.56],[94.56,95.16],[95.16,96.05],[96.05,97.08],[97.08,97.83],[98.09,98.23],[98.23,98.9],[98.9,99.57],[99.57,99.79],[99.79,100.02],[100.02,100.39],[100.39,100.61],[100.61,100.76],[100.76,101.35],[101.35,101.65],[101.65,102.32],[102.32,102.46],[102.46,102.69],[102.69,103.13]]},{"el":"h2","text":"Won again: Selma and the Voting Rights Act","start":104.61,"end":107.42,"words":[[104.66,104.88],[104.88,105.26],[105.53,105.9],[105.9,106.13],[106.13,106.28],[106.28,106.74],[106.74,107.11],[107.11,107.34]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Organizers registered voters at the risk of their lives.","start":108.12,"end":111.76,"words":[[108.17,108.83],[108.83,109.48],[109.48,109.92],[109.92,110.07],[110.07,110.22],[110.22,110.51],[110.51,110.65],[110.65,110.87],[110.87,111.31]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Medgar Evers was murdered in 1963, and three Freedom Summer workers, James Chaney, Andrew Goodman and Michael Schwerner, in 1964.","start":112.11,"end":121.8,"words":[[112.16,112.52],[112.52,112.8],[112.8,113.01],[113.01,113.37],[113.37,113.51],[113.51,114.71],[114.96,115.17],[115.17,115.38],[115.38,115.81],[115.81,116.09],[116.09,116.51],[116.76,117.18],[117.18,117.68],[117.93,118.28],[118.28,118.71],[118.71,118.92],[118.92,119.34],[119.34,119.77],[120.01,120.16],[120.16,121.29]]},{"el":"s2","text":"On March 7, 1965 (“Bloody Sunday”), state troopers beat marchers, including John Lewis, on the Edmund Pettus Bridge in Selma.","start":122.15,"end":132.93,"words":[[122.19,122.35],[122.35,122.69],[122.69,123.19],[123.49,124.91],[124.91,125.33],[125.33,125.92],[126.21,126.63],[126.63,127.13],[127.13,127.38],[127.38,127.97],[128.26,128.94],[128.94,129.27],[129.27,129.69],[129.98,130.15],[130.15,130.32],[130.32,130.82],[130.82,131.24],[131.24,131.66],[131.66,131.83],[131.83,132.33]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Five months later Congress passed the Voting Rights Act.","start":133.28,"end":136.68,"words":[[133.32,133.6],[133.6,133.95],[133.95,134.3],[134.3,134.8],[134.8,135.08],[135.08,135.22],[135.22,135.64],[135.64,135.99],[135.99,136.27]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Its Section 5 required states and counties with a history of discrimination to get federal approval before changing voting rules (“preclearance”).","start":137.03,"end":147.18,"words":[[137.08,137.32],[137.32,137.8],[137.8,138.13],[138.13,138.77],[138.77,139.25],[139.25,139.49],[139.49,140.05],[140.05,140.29],[140.29,140.45],[140.45,141.01],[141.01,141.17],[141.17,142.3],[142.3,142.46],[142.46,142.7],[142.7,143.26],[143.26,143.82],[143.82,144.14],[144.14,144.86],[144.86,145.35],[145.35,145.67],[145.67,146.63]]},{"el":"s2","text":"The 24th Amendment (1964) and a 1966 Supreme Court ruling ended poll taxes.","start":147.53,"end":155.44,"words":[[147.57,147.73],[147.73,148.45],[148.45,149.18],[149.18,150.38],[150.66,150.9],[150.9,151.06],[151.06,152.43],[152.43,152.91],[152.91,153.15],[153.15,153.56],[153.56,153.96],[153.96,154.28],[154.28,154.84]]},{"el":"s2","text":"In Mississippi, Black voter registration rose from about 7% in 1964 to about 60% by 1967.","start":155.79,"end":166.13,"words":[[155.83,155.99],[155.99,156.73],[157.01,157.34],[157.34,157.75],[157.75,158.81],[158.81,159.14],[159.14,159.47],[159.47,159.88],[159.88,160.78],[160.78,160.94],[160.94,162.17],[162.17,162.33],[162.33,162.74],[162.74,163.72],[163.72,163.97],[163.97,165.52]]},{"el":"h3","text":"Weakened: Shelby County v. Holder (2013)","start":167.03,"end":169.86,"words":[[167.08,167.44],[167.62,167.88],[167.88,168.19],[168.19,168.34],[168.6,168.91],[168.91,169.47]]},{"el":"s3","text":"In 2013 the Supreme Court struck down the formula that decided which places needed preclearance, which ended preclearance in practice.","start":170.56,"end":179.48,"words":[[170.6,170.75],[170.75,171.55],[171.55,171.7],[171.7,172.14],[172.14,172.35],[172.35,172.72],[172.72,173.01],[173.01,173.16],[173.16,173.67],[173.67,173.89],[173.89,174.47],[174.47,174.76],[174.76,175.27],[175.27,175.64],[175.64,176.51],[176.77,177.06],[177.06,177.43],[177.43,178.23],[178.23,178.38],[178.38,178.96]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Texas announced its voter ID law the same day.","start":179.83,"end":183.95,"words":[[179.89,180.44],[180.44,181.09],[181.09,181.36],[181.36,181.82],[181.82,182.19],[182.19,182.37],[182.37,182.56],[182.56,182.92],[182.92,183.29]]},{"el":"s3","text":"In 2016 a federal appeals court struck down a 2013 North Carolina law, finding that it targeted Black voters “with almost surgical precision.”","start":184.3,"end":195.22,"words":[[184.36,184.52],[184.52,185.56],[185.56,185.72],[185.72,186.28],[186.28,186.68],[186.68,186.92],[186.92,187.32],[187.32,187.64],[187.64,187.8],[187.8,188.68],[188.68,188.92],[188.92,189.64],[189.64,189.88],[190.16,190.72],[190.72,190.97],[190.97,191.13],[191.13,191.69],[191.69,192.01],[192.01,192.49],[192.49,192.73],[192.73,193.29],[193.29,193.93],[193.93,194.65]]},{"el":"s3","text":"A 2019 civil rights report counted about 1,700 polling places closed in formerly covered areas between 2012 and 2018.","start":195.57,"end":204.61,"words":[[195.61,195.74],[195.74,196.56],[196.56,196.87],[196.87,197.19],[197.19,197.5],[197.5,197.94],[197.94,198.26],[198.26,199.64],[199.64,200.02],[200.02,200.46],[200.46,200.84],[200.84,200.96],[200.96,201.34],[201.34,201.66],[201.66,202.03],[202.03,202.41],[202.41,203.1],[203.1,203.29],[203.29,204.11]]},{"el":"s3","text":"In 2023, in Allen v. Milligan, the Court did uphold Section 2 of the act and required Alabama to draw a second district where Black voters could elect their choice.","start":204.96,"end":217.73,"words":[[205.01,205.18],[205.18,206.5],[206.79,206.96],[206.96,207.29],[207.29,207.54],[207.96,208.62],[208.91,209.08],[209.08,209.33],[209.33,209.58],[209.58,210.16],[210.16,210.66],[210.66,210.82],[210.82,210.99],[210.99,211.16],[211.16,211.4],[211.4,211.65],[211.65,212.32],[212.32,212.9],[212.9,213.07],[213.07,213.31],[213.31,213.48],[213.48,213.98],[213.98,214.64],[214.64,214.89],[214.89,215.23],[215.23,215.72],[215.72,215.97],[215.97,216.39],[216.39,216.64],[216.64,217.14]]},{"el":"h4","text":"Locked out: felony disenfranchisement","start":218.63,"end":221.6,"words":[[218.68,218.99],[218.99,219.29],[219.56,220.02],[220.02,221.39]]},{"el":"s4","text":"About 4 million Americans can't vote because of a felony conviction, and Black Americans are disenfranchised at more than three times the rate of others (Sentencing Project).","start":222.3,"end":233.68,"words":[[222.34,222.74],[222.74,222.9],[222.9,223.38],[223.38,224.09],[224.09,224.41],[224.41,224.73],[224.73,225.12],[225.12,225.28],[225.28,225.44],[225.44,225.92],[225.92,226.71],[226.99,227.23],[227.23,227.55],[227.55,228.26],[228.26,228.34],[228.34,229.53],[229.53,229.69],[229.69,229.85],[229.85,230.09],[230.09,230.33],[230.33,230.73],[230.73,230.88],[230.88,231.2],[231.2,231.36],[231.36,231.68],[231.68,232.39],[232.39,233.11]]},{"el":"s4","text":"Many of these laws were written after Reconstruction to target crimes officials thought Black people were likelier to commit.","start":234.03,"end":241.22,"words":[[234.07,234.38],[234.38,234.53],[234.53,234.76],[234.76,234.99],[234.99,235.14],[235.14,235.52],[235.52,235.83],[235.83,236.82],[236.82,236.97],[236.97,237.35],[237.35,237.81],[237.81,238.34],[238.34,238.57],[238.57,238.88],[238.88,239.26],[239.26,239.41],[239.41,240.02],[240.02,240.17],[240.17,240.63]]},{"el":"s4","text":"In 1985 the Supreme Court struck down part of Alabama's law for that reason (Hunter v. Underwood).","start":241.57,"end":248.54,"words":[[241.62,241.77],[241.77,242.92],[242.92,243.07],[243.07,243.53],[243.53,243.76],[243.76,244.14],[244.14,244.44],[244.44,244.67],[244.67,244.83],[244.83,245.44],[245.44,245.59],[245.59,245.74],[245.74,245.97],[245.97,246.35],[246.35,246.74],[246.74,246.97],[247.35,247.96]]},{"el":"s4","text":"In 2018 Florida voters restored voting rights to most people who had finished their sentences; the legislature then required them to pay all fines and fees first.","start":248.89,"end":259.95,"words":[[248.94,249.1],[249.1,250.04],[250.04,250.59],[250.59,251.07],[251.07,251.54],[251.54,252.01],[252.01,252.4],[252.4,252.56],[252.56,252.95],[252.95,253.35],[253.35,253.5],[253.5,253.74],[253.74,254.21],[254.21,254.45],[254.45,255.23],[255.51,255.66],[255.66,256.53],[256.53,256.76],[256.76,257.39],[257.39,257.63],[257.63,257.79],[257.79,258.02],[258.02,258.18],[258.18,258.57],[258.57,258.81],[258.81,259.04],[259.04,259.44]]},{"el":"h5","text":"Drawn out: redistricting","start":260.85,"end":263.22,"words":[[260.86,261.23],[261.23,261.6],[261.93,263.05]]},{"el":"s5","text":"District lines can “pack” voters of color into a few districts or “crack” them across many so their votes count for less.","start":263.92,"end":270.59,"words":[[263.97,264.52],[264.52,264.86],[264.86,265.07],[265.07,265.28],[265.28,265.69],[265.69,265.83],[265.83,266.1],[266.1,266.38],[266.38,266.52],[266.52,266.72],[266.72,267.35],[267.35,267.41],[267.41,267.69],[267.69,267.9],[267.9,268.24],[268.24,268.52],[268.52,268.73],[268.73,268.93],[268.93,269.28],[269.28,269.62],[269.62,269.76],[269.76,270.04]]},{"el":"s5","text":"The Supreme Court ruled in 2019 that federal courts won't police partisan gerrymanders, but racial gerrymanders can still be challenged under the Voting Rights Act.","start":270.94,"end":281.31,"words":[[270.99,271.14],[271.14,271.57],[271.57,271.79],[271.79,272.08],[272.08,272.23],[272.23,273.17],[273.17,273.39],[273.39,273.9],[273.9,274.19],[274.19,274.56],[274.56,274.92],[274.92,275.43],[275.43,276.3],[276.56,276.78],[276.78,277.21],[277.21,278.02],[278.02,278.23],[278.23,278.52],[278.52,278.67],[278.67,279.33],[279.33,279.62],[279.62,279.76],[279.76,280.2],[280.2,280.56],[280.56,280.85]]},{"el":"s5","text":"Local offices matter as much as Congress: district attorneys, sheriffs and city councils decide how police calls are handled.","start":281.66,"end":289.25,"words":[[281.71,282.12],[282.12,282.53],[282.53,282.81],[282.81,282.95],[282.95,283.22],[283.22,283.36],[283.36,283.91],[284.15,284.7],[284.7,285.18],[285.42,285.84],[285.84,286.04],[286.04,286.32],[286.32,286.87],[286.87,287.28],[287.28,287.49],[287.49,287.83],[287.83,288.11],[288.11,288.18],[288.18,288.73]]}]}